\section{How something is deprecated}{

Nothing exported disappears without warning first.
\enumerate{
\item The function, argument, or returned field keeps working and says what to
use instead. An argument warns when it is passed; a returned field cannot
warn when it is read, so its documentation carries the notice instead.
\item The notice stands for \strong{at least one minor release}, so code has a
version in which it both runs and tells you what to change.
\item Removal follows: in a minor release before 1.0, and only in a major
release from 1.0 onward.
\item \code{NEWS.md} records both the deprecation and the removal, with the
replacement.
}

\code{anova_content()} supplies an example of each kind. Its \code{posthoc} argument
was deprecated in the first release, warned through every release to 0.6.0,
and was removed in 0.7.0. Its \code{posthoc_pass} returned column, a duplicate of
\code{contrast_pass}, could not warn when read, so it was documented as
deprecated in 0.7.0 and removed in 0.8.0. Both ends of each are recorded in
\code{NEWS.md}. No deprecation is in progress, so nothing is carried into 1.0.
}

\section{Changing a default}{

A changed default can silently change published numbers, so it is treated
as a breaking change even though no code fails. When a default changes, the
release notes say what changed, why the evidence supports it, and which
argument restores the previous behavior. Defaults are chosen from published
evidence; when that evidence is contested, the contested option is available
through an argument rather than made the default.
}

\section{The handoff schema}{

\code{\link[=content_handoff]{content_handoff()}} returns an exchange object that another package reads,
so it carries its own contract, agreed with the \code{nomologR} maintainers and
recorded at \url{https://github.com/JUhalt/nomologR/issues/46}.
\itemize{
\item Fields may be \strong{added} within a schema version. Existing fields keep
their names, types, and meanings.
\item Changing or removing a field, or changing what one means, requires a new
\code{schema_version}.
\item A reader checks that an optional field is present rather than assuming
it, and infers nothing from its absence beyond "this producer version did
not emit it".
\item The values in the \code{statistic} column are data rather than schema, and the
\code{note} column is display text. Both may be reworded in a minor release, so
neither should be matched on.
}
